  ('ai:ai-batch-03:5371c3a7c62d','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','According to the passage, what did Churchill''s speeches do?',array['They ended the war.','They won the 1945 election.','They created the NHS.','They raised public morale.']::text[],'D','His speeches ''helped to raise public morale''.',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in 1940, at a time when much of Europe had fallen to Nazi Germany. He refused to surrender, and his speeches, including the promise that ''we shall fight on the beaches'', helped to raise public morale. Although he was defeated in the general election of 1945, he returned as Prime Minister in 1951.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:70e90259be00','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What happened to Churchill in 1945?',array['He retired from politics for good.','He lost the general election.','He surrendered to Germany.','He became Prime Minister for the first time.']::text[],'B','''He was defeated in the general election of 1945.''',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in 1940, at a time when much of Europe had fallen to Nazi Germany. He refused to surrender, and his speeches, including the promise that ''we shall fight on the beaches'', helped to raise public morale. Although he was defeated in the general election of 1945, he returned as Prime Minister in 1951.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:bd3871bc0f1d','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Churchill: which statement is true according to the text?',array['He never lost an election.','He became Prime Minister after the war ended.','He supported surrender in 1940.','He served as Prime Minister on two separate occasions.']::text[],'D','1940 and again from 1951.',array['life_in_uk','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Winston Churchill became Prime Minister in 1940, at a time when much of Europe had fallen to Nazi Germany. He refused to surrender, and his speeches, including the promise that ''we shall fight on the beaches'', helped to raise public morale. Although he was defeated in the general election of 1945, he returned as Prime Minister in 1951.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d3874be62254','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','Which fuel powered the steam machines?',array['electricity','wood','coal','oil']::text[],'C','''Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel''.',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the 18th century, transformed the way goods were produced. Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel, replaced much work previously done by hand. Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities, where working conditions were often harsh, and children as well as adults worked long hours.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:6f570c52b074','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What social change does the passage describe?',array['people leaving cities for farms','people moving from the countryside to cities','fewer factories being built','shorter working hours for children']::text[],'B','''Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities.''',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the 18th century, transformed the way goods were produced. Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel, replaced much work previously done by hand. Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities, where working conditions were often harsh, and children as well as adults worked long hours.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:7e53c37c6380','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','In the passage on the Industrial Revolution, ''harsh'' is closest in meaning to ___.',array['unusual','severe','temporary','pleasant']::text[],'B','Harsh conditions are severe and unpleasant.',array['life_in_uk','world_knowledge','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','The Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the 18th century, transformed the way goods were produced. Machines powered by steam, using coal as fuel, replaced much work previously done by hand. Factories drew people from the countryside into rapidly growing cities, where working conditions were often harsh, and children as well as adults worked long hours.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:2375c0cbe652','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','How was the Citadel''s mound formed?',array['by layers of settlement built up over thousands of years','by a volcanic eruption','by river sediment','by a single king''s building project']::text[],'A','''Built up over thousands of years by successive layers of occupation.''',array['kurdistan','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Rising some 30 metres above the surrounding city, the Erbil Citadel is often described as one of the oldest continuously inhabited settlements in the world. Its oval mound has been built up over thousands of years by successive layers of occupation. In 2014 it was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, and restoration work has sought to preserve its traditional houses while making the site accessible to visitors.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:d180ef6ae107','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','When was the Erbil Citadel added to the UNESCO list?',array['in 2024','in 2004','in 2014','in 1988']::text[],'C','''In 2014 it was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List.''',array['kurdistan','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Rising some 30 metres above the surrounding city, the Erbil Citadel is often described as one of the oldest continuously inhabited settlements in the world. Its oval mound has been built up over thousands of years by successive layers of occupation. In 2014 it was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, and restoration work has sought to preserve its traditional houses while making the site accessible to visitors.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:efe2113ab883','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is the aim of the restoration work, according to the passage?',array['to preserve traditional houses while welcoming visitors','to replace old houses with modern ones','to close the site to the public','to build a new city on the mound']::text[],'A','''Preserve its traditional houses while making the site accessible to visitors.''',array['kurdistan','history','story']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Rising some 30 metres above the surrounding city, the Erbil Citadel is often described as one of the oldest continuously inhabited settlements in the world. Its oval mound has been built up over thousands of years by successive layers of occupation. In 2014 it was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, and restoration work has sought to preserve its traditional houses while making the site accessible to visitors.',null,null,null),
  ('ai:ai-batch-03:f654ac40b026','C1'::public.cefr_level,'reading','What is Paul''s main concern about entry fees?',array['that parks are too crowded','that local residents pay too much','that conservation is unnecessary','that they could exclude poorer families']::text[],'D','''Fees risk excluding families on low incomes.''',array['dialogue','world_knowledge']::text[],'draft','unreviewed','ai-draft:ai-batch-03','Presenter: Should national parks charge entry fees?
